Q: Why does Combine.PassthroughSubject drop values sent synchronously in handleEvents(receiveSubscription:)? A: <p>Fundamentally, the problem is that, as the <a href="https://developer.apple.com/documentation/combine/passthroughsubject" rel="nofollow noreferrer">docs</a> tell us (emphasis added):</p>
<blockquote>
<p>A <code>PassthroughSubject</code> drops values if there are no subscribers, or <em>its current demand is zero.</em></p>
</blockquote>
<p>So sending values inside the <code>receiveSubscription</code> closure is just too early in the process. The demand is not established until the <code>sink</code>. I’m not sure why you’re using <code>handleEvents</code> at all. Usually one creates the publisher (the <code>PassThroughSubject</code> in this case), add the <code>sink</code>, and only then send values/completion.</p>
<p>I am unaware of any formal assurances that <code>sink</code>  would somehow defer <code>receiveSubscription</code> until after it had also demanded any values. And your example perfectly illustrates that, in practice, this is not the case. It apparently creates a subscriber and attaches it to the publisher (resulting in <code>receiveSubscription</code> being called) and only then demands values. I hear you, that this might not have been what you expected, but this is how it works.</p>
<hr />
<p>In practice, we would generally do:</p>
<pre class="lang-swift prettyprint-override"><code>import XCTest

class CombineSubscriptionTest: XCTestCase {
    func testSendingInReceiveSubscription() {
        let value = 1

        let subject = PassthroughSubject&lt;Int, Never&gt;()

        let expectation = expectation(description: &quot;test&quot;)
        var collectedValues = [Int]()

        let cancellable = subject.sink { completion in
            expectation.fulfill()
        } receiveValue: { value in
            collectedValues.append(value)
        }

        subject.send(value)
        subject.send(completion: .finished)

        wait(for: [expectation], timeout: 1.0)

        XCTAssertEqual(collectedValues, [value])
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Or if one is just stubbing a <code>Publisher</code> for the sake of a unit test, consider <a href="https://developer.apple.com/documentation/combine/just/" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>Just</code></a>:</p>
<pre class="lang-swift prettyprint-override"><code>import XCTest

class CombineSubscriptionTest: XCTestCase {
    func testSendingInReceiveSubscription() {
        let value = 1

        let publisher = Just(value)

        let expectation = expectation(description: &quot;test&quot;)
        var collectedValues = [Int]()

        let cancellable = publisher.sink { completion in
            expectation.fulfill()
        } receiveValue: { value in
            collectedValues.append(value)
        }

        wait(for: [expectation], timeout: 1.0)

        XCTAssertEqual(collectedValues, [value])
    }
}
</code></pre>

Q: How can I write if-else conditions in cypress according to element&#39;s value? A: <p>The problem is both switches have the same <code>data-test</code>, so Cypress starts to get confused</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>cy.get('[data-test=&quot;form-switch&quot;]')
  .eq(0)                              // pick one
  .invoke('attr','aria-checked')      // this attr indicates checked state
  .then(($switchOnOff) =&gt; {

    if($switchOnOff === 'true') {  // note adjustments here

      cy.isContain('.item-undefined-switch[data-test=&quot;item-undefined-email&quot;]', 'true')  
    } else {
      cy.isContain('.item-undefined-switch[data-test=&quot;item-undefined-email&quot;]', 'false')
    }
})
</code></pre>
<p>Or all switches</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>cy.get('[data-test=&quot;form-switch&quot;]')
  .each(($switchOnOff) =&gt; {
    const value = $switchOnOff.attr('aria-checked')
    cy.isContain('.item-undefined-switch[data-test=&quot;item-undefined-email&quot;]', value)  
})
</code></pre>
